The setcookie() function creates a cookie.
Basic syntax: setcookie('name', 'value', expiration);
Example: setcookie('my_cookie', '123456', time() + 3600);
This creates a cookie named 'my_cookie' with value '123456' that expires in 1 hour.
time() returns the current timestamp; you add the desired number of seconds.

Use the $_COOKIE superglobal (an associative array):
  if (isset($_COOKIE['my_cookie'])) {
      $value = $_COOKIE['my_cookie'];
  }
You must check with isset() because the cookie may not exist (first visit, expired, etc.) — reading a missing key would cause an error.
You can only read cookies that belong to the current domain.

You cannot directly delete the file (it lives on the client). Instead, you 'recreate' the cookie with a past expiration and an empty value:
  setcookie('my_cookie', '', 1);
You use '1' (a guaranteed past timestamp) rather than time() - 3600 because time() depends on the server's clock. If the server clock is wrong, the deletion will fail. '1' always ensures the cookie is expired.
